---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHero from "../../components/PageHero.astro";
import CompareTable from "../../components/CompareTable.astro";
import SiteCheckForm from "../../components/SiteCheckForm.astro";

const rows = [
  { label: "Puts a visible mark on product images", them: "Yes", us: "-" },
  { label: "Invisible to customers", them: "No", us: "Yes" },
  { label: "Travels with the content when it is copied", them: "Images only", us: "Yes" },
  { label: "Proves a stolen description was yours", them: "No", us: "Yes" },
  { label: "Produces evidence for a DMCA takedown", them: "No", us: "Yes" },
];
---

<Base
  title="Visible watermarks vs Kirpik"
  description="A plain comparison of visible image watermarks with Kirpik: what a stamped mark does for product photos, whether it prevents content theft, and what protects the text beside the images."
>
  <PageHero
    title="Visible watermarks vs Kirpik"
    lead="Several apps in Shopify's anti-theft category stamp a logo, name or badge onto product images. A visible mark travels with the image wherever it is copied; the text beside the image is out of its scope."
  />

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="container narrow prose">
      <h2>What a visible watermark does</h2>
      <p>
        These apps lay a visible mark over your product photos, usually in bulk, keeping the originals
        so the mark can be removed later. Because the mark is part of the image file, it stays in place
        when the image is saved, screenshotted or re-uploaded: the mark travels with the image.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two boundaries follow. The mark can be taken off: a corner logo crops out, and image-editing
        tools can reconstruct the picture beneath a mark laid across the frame. And it is an image
        technique only. Your product descriptions, blog articles and pages have no pixels to carry a
        mark, and there is no visible equivalent for text: anything a reader can see in text, an editor
        can delete.
      </p>

      <h2>Does it prevent content theft?</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>No.</strong> A watermarked image can still be saved and reposted; the mark identifies
        the image but does not stop the taking. For text the technique is out of scope entirely: it does
        not stop Shopify serving your descriptions through the machine-readable feeds (JSON, XML and
        ATOM) that scrapers actually read, and it leaves the words on the page untouched.
      </p>
      <p>
        Kirpik does not claim to prevent content theft: it <strong>protects</strong> content. The
        watermark travels inside the text itself, so stolen content stays identifiable and provable
        wherever it surfaces. Kirpik adds no scripts to your storefront, so it runs alongside any
        blocker without conflict.
      </p>

      <h2>Can it prove infringement?</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>No.</strong> A visible mark puts your name on the image, but the listings in this group
        describe no detection and no ownership document, and for the text on the page a stamped logo
        does nothing.
      </p>
      <p>
        Kirpik proves infringement through the watermark and the Proof of Ownership Certificate.
        Detection reads your watermark out of the stolen text, and the certificate records dated
        fingerprints, the infringing page's timestamps, Wayback Machine corroboration and a public
        verification page, ready for a DMCA takedown.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <h2 class="tablehead">Visible watermarks vs Kirpik</h2>
      <CompareTable themLabel="Visible watermarks" rows={rows} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <SiteCheckForm tone="band" heading="Is your store's content protected?" cta="Check my store">
    <Fragment slot="sub">
      Do you use <strong>a visible image watermark</strong> or any other "Content Protection" App?
      Enter your Shopify store address to see if your content is actually protected.
    </Fragment>
  </SiteCheckForm>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <section class="fineprint">
        <p>
          This page describes visible image watermarking as implemented across the Shopify anti-theft
          category, per each app's own public App Store listing as recorded in June and July 2026;
          listings change. Removal notes describe what cropping and image-editing tools do in general,
          not defects of any app. App-by-app pages are in
          <a href="/compare/">the full comparison survey</a>. App names and trademarks belong to their
          respective owners and are used for identification only. Nothing here is legal advice.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .tablehead { font-size: clamp(1.45rem, 1.15rem + 1.2vw, 2rem); }
  .fineprint { padding-top: 1.4rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .fineprint p { font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--ink-faint); line-height: 1.65; max-width: 78ch; }
  .fineprint a { color: var(--orange-deep); font-weight: 600; }
</style>
